 "edit_calls": 1, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "final_workspace": "/workspace/openbench-ab/results/step5-opus-aa-1017-omp36-aa/omp-36/cells/aa-1/trig-omp-reported/10/workspace", "first_after_build": false, "first_side": "aa-2", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "omp/17.3.8 (/workspace/openbench-ab/results/omp-cache/bin/omp/5347ff91e8f44f2c69a79d7b152b7301687cd5c8/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "`test_dates` passes now. The old `strptime` format string had no way to read a timezone suffix, so it failed on the trailing `Z`. In `dates/parse.py`, `parse_date` now uses `datetime.fromisoformat(s)`, which reads `Z` as UTC on Python 3.11 and later. This environment runs 3.13.5.\n\nAll 3 tests in `tests/test_parse.py` pass, including `test_naive` and `test_day_key`.\n\nTimestamps ending in `Z` (or another offset) now come back timezone-aware, set to UTC. Timestamps with no offset still come back without a timezone, as before. If any caller compares or subtracts the two kinds, Python raises a `TypeError`. The tests don't do that, but other callers might. `day_key` is unaffected because it only formats the date.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "params_stripped": [], "params_stripped_requests": 0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 2, "replies_ok": 5, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 51469, "requests_cache_write": 78321, "requests_cost_usd": 0.4222668, "requests_count": 5, "requests_input_uncached": 12, "requests_output": 1016, "reread": false, "rule_order": "none", "rule_prefix": "neither", "r